{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of light that only exists in late October. It comes in low and golden, cutting sideways through the trees, making even the most ordinary street look like a film set. I've always thought autumn is the most honest season. Spring tries too hard to impress you with blossoms and promises, summer boasts, winter sulks, but autumn simply tells the truth: things change, things fall, and there is beauty in that.\n\nI like to walk without a destination in that light. No podcasts, no phone calls, just the crunch of leaves under my boots and the cool air on my face. People rush past, wrapped in scarves, holding coffees like warm anchors. Everyone is heading somewhere, and for a moment I envy their certainty. Then I remember that wandering is also a kind of destination.\n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to be efficient. Optimizing our sleep, our work, our conversations. But some things aren't meant to be optimized. A slow conversation with an old friend that goes nowhere and everywhere. A book you read not because it will make you smarter but because the sentences feel good in your mouth. A walk you take with no step count.\n\nMaybe the point isn't to arrive. Maybe the point is to notice the light while it lasts. It will fade by November, return next year, and in between we learn again how to pay attention. That, I think, is enough for one lifetime. Really enough.",
